############ Reading — exactly what this log establishes and what it does not ############
# PROVEN (a): ts seeded .release-please-manifest.json with {".": "0.1.0"} at commit 19ff9b5, and the first
#   release-please release commit that followed was bdc7567, 'chore(release): publish v0.1.1'. The seeded
#   number 0.1.0 never appears as a release commit.
# PROVEN (b): the clone carries tags ts-launch-blueprint-v0.1.1, v0.1.2 and v0.1.3. There is no v0.1.0 tag.
# PROVEN (c): npmjs.com serves the package name ts-launch-blueprint (HTTP 200) with exactly two versions,
#   0.0.1 (2026-07-21, published by the user account 'smorin', no repository field — a name reservation)
#   and 0.1.3 (2026-07-22, published by 'GitHub Actions' through a trusted publisher, repository
#   git+https://github.com/smorinlabs/ts-launch-blueprint.git). dist-tags.latest = 0.1.3.
#   0.1.0, 0.1.1 and 0.1.2 are ABSENT from the registry: 'npm view ts-launch-blueprint@0.1.1' exits 1 with
#   E404. So ts's first pipeline publication was 0.1.3, three release commits after its first release
#   commit. D-021(5) explains the gap: the publish workflow shipped 'inert-but-ready until the consumer
#   configures the npmjs.com trusted publisher'.
# THEREFORE SUPPORTS: the source-level inference that release-please reads a seeded manifest entry as an
#   ALREADY-RELEASED version, so the seeded number is skipped by the next Release PR. Every one of (a),
#   (b) and (c) shows 0.1.0 absent, which is consistent with that inference.
# CANNOT SUPPORT: any claim that ts published 0.1.1, or that a release commit or tag implies a registry
#   publication. The authority for the empty-manifest recommendation is the release-please source read at
#   v17.11.2 (Manifest's 'backfill latest release tags from manifest' block plus
#   BaseStrategy.buildNewVersion); ts's history is corroboration of that source reading, never a substitute
#   for it, and it says nothing about crates.io.
